<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fiesole</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fiesole is a hill town overlooking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> in Tuscany, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a> and later a Roman town, it was older than Florence and long its rival, until the Florentines conquered and destroyed it in 1125. It preserves a Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and Etruscan walls, and its villas and views made it a favourite retreat of wealthy Florentines.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fiesole.jpg" alt="Fiesole" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hagai Agmon-Snir حچاي اچمون-سنير חגי אגמון-שניר · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FiesoleDec102023_15.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Fiesole is a town and comune of the Metropolitan City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> in Tuscany, on a height above Florence, about 5 km north-east of that city. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> name was Faesulae, from the Etruscan Viesul, Viśl or Vipsul. It was an important member of the Etruscan confederacy, and the remains of its ancient walls and buildings have been preserved, with an archaeological museum in the town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest recorded mention dates to 283 BCE, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> conquered the Etruscan town. In Roman times it was the seat of a school of augurs, to which twelve young men were sent each year from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to study divination. The old town was destroyed either in the Social War or by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> in 80 BCE, in reprisal for supporting the populares, and Sulla colonised it with veterans, who later supported Catilina under Gaius Mallius. In 406 the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stilicho">Stilicho</a> won a victory near Fiesole over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suebi">Suebi</a> under Radagaisus. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothic-war-535-554">Gothic War</a> the town was besieged several times, and in 539 the Byzantine general Justin captured it and razed its fortifications.</p><p>For several centuries in the early Middle Ages Fiesole was independent and no less powerful than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> in the valley below, and wars arose between them. The Florentines sacked it in 1010 and 1025 and conquered it in 1125, obliging its leading families to live in Florence. Dante reflects the rivalry in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divine-comedy">Divine Comedy</a> by referring to the beasts of Fiesole. From the 14th century wealthy Florentines had villas in the hills, and one is the setting of the frame story of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-boccaccio">Boccaccio</a>'s Decameron. The Medici built a villa there in the fifteenth century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Roman theatre, below the cathedral, has 19 tiers of stone seats and is 34 metres in diameter, and there are remains of Roman baths and of Etruscan walls. The cathedral holds the shrine of Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a>, according to legend the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, and an altarpiece by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perugino">Pietro Perugino</a>. The Badia, the old cathedral, was built in 1028. The European University Institute is based in the town, in historic buildings such as the Badia Faesolina and the Villa Schifanoia, and several American universities maintain campuses there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fiesole” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Like other Italian communes, Florence then set out to dominate its countryside, the contado: it destroyed the hill town of <b>Fiesole</b> in 1125, forced rural lords to live in the city for part of the year, and fought Siena, Pisa, Lucca and Pistoia for roads and markets.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
